    "data": {
      "A": "Research reusable position storage, not further scalar target tiling. The measured packed snapshot was neutral, while scalar four-target tiling regressed substantially. Explore allocating x/y/z arrays once and refreshing them alongside the existing position-update pass, eliminating per-call allocation and separate per-step packing. Keep positions immutable during force evaluation, use checked allocation with a correct fallback, and preserve binary I/O. Deliver this as an optional layout change compatible with the SIMD and threaded kernels; retain it only if end-to-end integration measurements justify the extra storage and writes.",
      "B": "Use four-target NEON program f3ec2729e1fe4f2e as the SIMD reference. Research two-source unrolling/software pipelining to expose independent double-precision square roots and divisions, while adding contributions in ascending source order to one accumulator per target. Preserve the accepted arithmetic sequence and contraction behavior; do not introduce approximate reciprocals, sqrtf, horizontal reductions, or reassociation. Handle odd source counts and incomplete target blocks exactly. Supply a non-threaded four-target helper for G. Treat the slower eight-target program as an alternative requiring new integration evidence, not an automatic improvement.",
      "C": "Use persistent-team program 582f183186464085 as the threading reference. Preserve static target ownership and both barriers: all old-position reads must finish before integration, and all new positions must be published before the next force phase. Research a genuine serial timestep path below the crossover instead of executing serialized OpenMP worksharing constructs. Retain the existing cutoff unless authorized measurements support changing it. Keep the one-barrier ping-pong program 952b777044dd4b83 available as an alternative, but its nearly identical measured time does not establish a synchronization advantage. Keep all work within the fixed four-core execution configuration.",
      "G": "Preserve 582f183186464085 unchanged as the measured incumbent. First integrate its persistent OpenMP team with B's four-target NEON kernel. Statically distribute target blocks through one force worksharing loop; use scalar handling for the final incomplete block, with every target processed exactly once. Helpers must not create nested teams. Retain the force-to-integration barrier and next-timestep barrier. Next, if the existing budget permits, compare four-target NEON with C's ping-pong variant, keeping read/write buffers distinct and pointer swaps thread-local after the worksharing barrier. Only afterward evaluate one A or B refinement at a time. Validate and measure each complete integrated program using the unchanged harness. Compare against the incumbent and relevant component programs; keep the fastest correct measured artifact, including unchanged C if combinations regress. Do not infer additive or multiplicative gains.",
      "analysis": "Only the supplied n-body program is relevant: ten all-pairs timesteps on ARM64, four CPU cores, no GPU. Baseline time is 0.663898 s. Persistent-team C reaches 0.170113 s, about 3.90x faster; ping-pong C takes 0.170683 s, essentially tied without further timing evidence. Four-target NEON B takes 0.326810 s, about 2.03x faster; eight targets take 0.335879 s. Packed-position A is neutral at 0.664086 s; scalar target tiling regresses to 0.926589 s. These measurements prioritize testing threading plus four-target SIMD, not assuming it wins. All roles must preserve ascending-j float accumulation, self interactions, float distance arithmetic followed by double sqrt/division and conversion to float, and velocity-before-position updates. Preserve ten iterations, constants, input count plus six-float records, and output records without a count header. Existing admissibility is evidence, not proof for every valid input; retain general bounds, tails, and correctness-preserving fallbacks. Tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, and permissions remain unchanged."
    },
